  "summary": "A style characterized by bold, non-naturalistic colors and expressive brushwork.",
  "description": "Fauvism emerged from artists like Henri Matisse and André Derain who used intense color to express emotion rather than depict reality. The movement rejected traditional perspective and muted palettes in favor of vivid, often clashing hues. Its name, 'wild beasts,' was coined by critics who were shocked by the radical departure from academic conventions.",
  "culturalContext": "Fauvism (1904-1908) — Matisse, Derain, Vlaminck at Collioure — detonated color from description: green stripes on faces (\"don't put nature in your pictures\"), vermilion skies, complementary explosions at full tube strength. Named \"wild beasts\" (fauves) by critic Louis Vauxcelles at the 1905 Salon d'Automne, the movement lasted barely four years but permanently freed Western painting from naturalistic color, feeding Matisse's lifelong decorative mastery, German Expressionism, and every poster-colored style since.",
